# Sources for religious and bazaar networks in the coalition: Fakhreddin
# Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, 1941–1953, chs. 5–6,
# especially pp. 250–267; Painter and Brew, The Struggle for Iran, ch. 1.
# The recurring outreach and its effects are counterfactual.
title: Bazaar Outreach
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: public_campaign
view-if: bazaar_outreach_timer <= 0
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; bazaar_outreach_timer = 4

= Bazaar Outreach

Bazaar and {!<span class="term term-religious">!}religious networks{!</span>!} can carry a political appeal through
relationships the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front's{!</span>!}
formal committees do not command. Outreach must be
negotiated with those networks, and the language chosen will determine which
part of the coalition feels represented.

The secretariat can foreground oil, constitutional government, or the
practical independence of the cooperating networks.

- @bazaar_oil
- @bazaar_constitution
- @bazaar_autonomy

@bazaar_oil
title: Organize around national control of oil
subtitle: Oil supplies a common language for merchants, clerics, and nationalists
on-arrival: religious_network_relation += 4; religious_network_organization += 4; oil_coalition_support += 3

Intermediaries carry an oil appeal through commercial and religious contacts,
adapting the language to meetings the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} itself could not convene. The
campaign gains organizers as well as listeners.

Cooperation around oil becomes more practical. Those networks still answer to
their own leaders rather than to the secretariat.

- @post_event: Continue

@bazaar_constitution
title: Organize around constitutional government
subtitle: Constitutional government offers cooperation without asking the <span class="term term-religious">religious network</span> to surrender its own authority
on-arrival: religious_network_relation += 3; religious_network_dissent -= 2; constitutional_legitimacy += 2; organizational_reach += 2

The appeal stresses lawful government and representative institutions rather
than asking every participant to endorse a broader social program.
{!<span class="term term-religious">!}Religious-network{!</span>!} organizers can carry that case without surrendering their
own political identity.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} reaches new
listeners and reduces immediate suspicion, though it
does less this month to consolidate a detailed oil position.

- @post_event: Continue

@bazaar_autonomy
title: Negotiate an autonomous joint committee
subtitle: A joint committee bargains as two partners rather than speaking with one voice
on-arrival: religious_network_relation += 5; religious_network_dissent -= 4

The secretariat meets intermediaries privately and agrees on a channel for
sharing plans without placing their organizations under
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} discipline.
Each side explains where cooperation is possible and where it would be refused.

The coalition gains a more reliable working relationship and fewer surprises.
It gains less immediate public pressure than a full campaign appeal.

- @post_event: Continue
